question:
Find all functions $f: \mathbb{R}^{+} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^{+}$ such that

$$
f(x+f(y))=f(x+y)+f(y)\tag{1}
$$

for all $x, y \in \mathbb{R}^{+}$. (Symbol $\mathbb{R}^{+}$denotes the set of all positive real numbers.)

response:
First, we see that $f(1)$ must be $1$, since

\begin{align*}
f\left(f(x)^{2}\right) &= f\left(f(x)^{2} \cdot 1\right) = x^{3} f(x) \\
f\left(f(1)^{2} x\right) &= f\left(f(1) \cdot f(1) x\right) = 1^{3} f(x) = f(x)
\end{align*}

Thus, $f(x) = f\left(f(1)^{2} x\right)$ for all $x$, so $f(1)^{2} = 1$. Since $f(1) \in \mathbb{Q}^{+}$, we have $f(1) = 1$.

Now, let $x = 1$ in (1) to get

\begin{align*}
f\left(f(y)\right) &= f\left(f(1)^{2} y\right) = 1^{3} f(y) = f(y)
\end{align*}

So, $f$ is an injective function.

Since $f$ is injective, we can apply $f$ to both sides of (1) to get

\begin{align*}
f\left(f\left(f(x)^{2} y\right)\right) &= f\left(x^{3} f(x y)\right) \\
f\left(x^{3} f(x y)\right) &= f\left(f(x)^{2} y\right)
\end{align*}

Now, let $y = 1$ in (1) to get

\begin{align*}
f\left(f(x)^{2}\right) &= x^{3} f(x)
\end{align*}

Substituting $f(x) = x^{3} f(x)$ into the equation above, we get

\begin{align*}
f\left(x^{3} f(x)\right) &= f\left(f(x)^{2}\right) = x^{3} f(x)
\end{align*}

Since $f$ is injective, we can conclude that $f(x) = x$ for all $x$.
</think>

<answer>\boxed{f(x)=x \text{ for all } x \in \mathbb{Q}^{+}}</answer>

We claim that $k=n^2$ is the smallest possible number of marked cells.

To see that this number is sufficient, consider the following marking:

[asy]
unitsize(1cm);

int n = 5;

for(int i = 1; i <= 2*n; ++i) {
  draw((i-1,0)--(i,0));
  draw((0,i-1)--(0,i));
}

for(int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
  fill((i-1,2*i-1)--(i,2*i-1)--cycle, gray);
  fill((i-1,2*i)--(i,2*i)--cycle, gray);
}

[/asy]

To see that this is the smallest possible number, we show that if $k < n^2$ cells are marked, then there are at least two ways to partition the board into dominoes so that none of them contains two marked cells.

Let $B$ be the $2n \times 2n$ board. We can divide $B$ into four $n \times n$ boards, one in each corner. We can also divide $B$ into four $2n \times n$ boards, two in the top and two in the bottom.

If any of the four $n \times n$ boards has no marked cells, then we can partition that board into dominoes, and the remaining $2n \times 2n - n \times n = 3n^2$ cells can be partitioned into $2n \times 2n$ dominoes. This gives two ways to partition the board.

If each of the four $n \times n$ boards has at least one marked cell, then since there are $k < n^2$ marked cells in total, at least one of the four $2n \times n$ boards has no marked cells. We can partition that board into dominoes, and the remaining $2n \times 2n - 2n \times n = 2n^2$ cells can be partitioned into $2n \times 2n$ dominoes. This gives two ways to partition the board.

Thus $k=n^2$ is the smallest possible number.
</think>

