Suppose that, for some angles $x$ and $y$,

$$
\begin{aligned}
& \sin ^{2} x+\cos ^{2} y=\frac{3}{2} a \\
& \cos ^{2} x+\sin ^{2} y=\frac{1}{2} a^{2}
\end{aligned}
$$

Determine the possible value(s) of $a$.

response:
We have
\begin{align*}
\cos \left(\frac{3}{2} \cos x\right) &= \sin \left(\frac{3}{2} \sin x\right) \\
\cos \left(\frac{3}{2} \cos x\right) &= \cos \left(\frac{\pi}{2} - \frac{3}{2} \sin x\right)
\end{align*}
Thus, either
\begin{align*}
\frac{3}{2} \cos x &= \frac{\pi}{2} - \frac{3}{2} \sin x \\
\cos x + \sin x &= \frac{\pi}{3}
\end{align*}
or
\begin{align*}
\frac{3}{2} \cos x &= -\left(\frac{\pi}{2} - \frac{3}{2} \sin x\right) \\
\cos x - \sin x &= -\frac{\pi}{3}
\end{align*}
In the first case, we have
\begin{align*}
\sin 2x &= 2 \sin x \cos x \\
&= 2 \left(\frac{\pi}{3} - \sin x\right) \sin x \\
&= 2 \left(\frac{\pi^2}{9} - \frac{\pi}{3} \sin x + \sin^2 x\right)
\end{align*}
Since $0 < x < \frac{\pi}{2}$, we know that $0 < \sin x < 1$.  The quadratic $2 \left(\frac{\pi^2}{9} - \frac{\pi}{3} \sin x + \sin^2 x\right)$ has a maximum value of $\frac{\pi^2}{2}$ when $\sin x = \frac{\pi}{6}$, and a minimum value of $2 \left(\frac{\pi^2}{9} - \frac{\pi}{3}\right)$ when $\sin x = 1$.  Therefore, we have
\begin{align*}
2 \left(\frac{\pi^2}{9} - \frac{\pi}{3}\right) &< \sin 2x < \frac{\pi^2}{2} \\
\frac{2 \pi^2 - 6 \pi}{9} &< \sin 2x < \frac{\pi^2}{2}
\end{align*}
In the second case, we have
\begin{align*}
\sin 2x &= 2 \sin x \cos x \\
&= 2 \left(\frac{\pi}{3} + \sin x\right) \sin x \\
&= 2 \left(\frac{\pi^2}{9} + \frac{\pi}{3} \sin x + \sin^2 x\right)
\end{align*}
Since $0 < x < \frac{\pi}{2}$, we know that $0 < \sin x < 1$.  The quadratic $2 \left(\frac{\pi^2}{9} + \frac{\pi}{3} \sin x + \sin^2 x\right)$ has a minimum value of $\frac{\pi^2}{2}$ when $\sin x = \frac{\pi}{6}$, and a maximum value of $2 \left(\frac{\pi^2}{9} + \frac{\pi}{3}\right)$ when $\sin x = 1$.  Therefore, we have
\begin{align*}
\frac{\pi^2}{2} &< \sin 2x < 2 \left(\frac{\pi^2}{9} + \frac{\pi}{3}\right) \\
\frac{\pi^2}{2} &< \sin 2x < \frac{2 \pi^2 + 6 \pi}{9}
\end{align*}
Combining these two cases, we have
\begin{align*}
\frac{2 \pi^2 - 6 \pi}{9} &< \sin 2x < \frac{2 \pi^2 + 6 \pi}{9}
\end{align*}
Thus, the possible values of $\sin 2x$ are $\boxed{\frac{2 \pi^2 - 6 \pi}{9}}$ and $\boxed{\frac{2 \pi^2 + 6 \pi}{9}}$.
